{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cfun35YxSxjmtSwizcc3e","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**Partially, depending on your Wrangler version.**\n\n- **KV namespaces:** Recent Wrangler 4.x versions support automatic resource provisioning. If a KV binding in `wrangler.toml`/`wrangler.jsonc` omits the `id`, `wrangler deploy` creates the namespace and binds it. This also applies to R2 buckets and D1 databases. Older versions require you to run `wrangler kv namespace create <name>` first and paste the returned `id` into your config.\n- **Queues:** These are not auto-created. If a producer or consumer binding references a queue that doesn't exist, the deploy fails. Create it first with `wrangler queues create <name>`.\n\nCheck `wrangler --version` and the changelog for your release to confirm. Pinning explicit IDs in your config is still the safest option for production, since it ensures each environment binds to the exact resource you expect."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2252,"cache_read_input_tokens":535,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2252},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5f3a666d-346e-4d08-994f-54e907ceab36","uuid":"7a110f59-5dac-4fb8-a78b-c35da48a352f","timestamp":"2026-10-11T03:57:00.574Z","request_id":"req_011Cfun35MJbHQ2pBJsZa24K"}
